// Approved simple entrance; existing menu and pages remain available.
PUBLIC_PAGES["/entrance.html"] = "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\"><title>The Greatest Love Story Ever Told \u2014 A Simple Beginning \u00b7 Our Shared Journey</title><meta name=\"description\" content=\"The Creator of the universe wants a personal relationship with you. A simple beginning to Our Shared Journey.\"><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f8f6f0;color:#272823;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif}main{max-width:730px;margin:auto;padding:clamp(36px,6vw,64px) clamp(22px,6vw,70px)}.brand{text-align:center;color:#7e7566;font:11px system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:3px;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:34px}h1{font-size:clamp(31px,5vw,44px);line-height:1.13;letter-spacing:-1px;text-align:center;font-weight:normal;margin:0 0 12px}.subtitle{text-align:center;color:#7d746a;font-style:italic;font-size:17px;margin:0 0 38px}.story{font-size:17px;line-height:1.8}.story p{margin:0 0 20px}a{color:#5e513c;text-underline-offset:5px}a:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #85613a;outline-offset:6px}.continue{display:block;text-align:center;margin:34px 0;font-size:18px}footer{text-align:center;border-top:1px solid #dcd2c4;padding:24px 0;font:13px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}footer a{margin:0 10px}</style></head><body><main><div class=\"brand\">Our Shared Journey</div><h1>The Greatest Love Story<br>Ever Told</h1><p class=\"subtitle\">A Simple Beginning</p><div class=\"story\"><p>There is a God who created everything. Everything we can see, everything we cannot see, and things we cannot even begin to imagine.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because God is Love.</p>\n<p>He created mankind to share in that Love, to know Him, to walk with Him, and to enjoy a relationship with Him.</p>\n<p>Something happened that disrupted that relationship. We chose our own way.</p>\n<p>God did not abandon us. He loved us so much that He gave everything Heaven had to restore what had been lost.</p>\n<p>Think about that. The Creator of the universe wants a personal relationship with you.</p>\n<p>We struggle to believe something so enormous because we want to understand it. We have these remarkable little brains, weighing about three pounds, and we imagine that somehow we ought to be able to comprehend the One who created the universe.</p>\n<p>We cannot even imagine the size of the questions, much less understand all the answers.</p>\n<p>Perhaps we have been approaching this backward.</p>\n<p>What if we began by simply believing that He is, that He loves us, and that He wants us to know Him?</p>\n<p>Then we could spend eternity discovering just how wonderful He really is.</p>\n<p><strong>That is the story. That is the invitation.</strong></p>\n<p>Now, what will you do with that?</p></div><a class=\"continue\" href=\"/index.html#invitation-title\">Continue to The Invitation \u2192</a><footer><a href=\"/index.html\">Main Menu</a><a href=\"/testimonies\">Our Shared Journey</a></footer></main></body></html>";
// Approved Dawn Key story and Scripture references.
PUBLIC_PAGES["/the-wisdom-of-dawn-key"] = "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"><title>The Wisdom of Dawn Key \u00b7 Our Shared Journey</title><style>\n:root{color-scheme:light;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;color:#29382f;background:#faf8f2}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0}header,main,footer{max-width:760px;margin:auto;padding:28px 24px}header{border-bottom:1px solid #d9ded4;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;align-items:center}header a{font-size:16px}a{color:#305d45;text-underline-offset:4px}h1{font-size:clamp(32px,6vw,46px);font-weight:normal;line-height:1.15;margin:16px 0 24px}h2{font-size:25px;font-weight:normal}p,li{font-size:19px;line-height:1.7}.eyebrow,.notice,footer,.count{font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:13px;line-height:1.6}.eyebrow{letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#5a6d5f}.notice{padding:12px 16px;background:#eeeade;border-radius:4px;margin-bottom:32px}.placeholder{padding:24px;border:1px dashed #879887;background:#f1f3ec;margin:24px 0;border-radius:5px}.placeholder p{font-size:17px;margin-bottom:0}.media{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:16px}.media .placeholder{margin:0;min-height:145px}nav.reading{margin-top:40px;border-top:1px solid #d9ded4;padding-top:24px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:24px}.next{display:inline-block;padding:13px 19px;background:#305d45;color:white;border-radius:4px;text-decoration:none;font-family:system-ui,sans-serif;font-size:15px}.menu-list{padding-left:24px}.menu-list li{padding:10px 0}footer{color:#667065;border-top:1px solid #d9ded4;margin-top:32px}.count{color:#667065}a:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #ab753a;outline-offset:5px}@media(max-width:520px){.media{grid-template-columns:1fr}nav.reading{align-items:flex-start;flex-direction:column}}[hidden]{display:none!important}\n</style></head><body><header><span>Speak I Hear &middot; His Love Story</span><nav aria-label=\"Main navigation\"><a href=\"/index.html\">Main Menu</a> &middot; <a href=\"/testimonies\">Our Shared Journey</a></nav></header><main><span class=\"eyebrow\">Our Shared Journey</span><h1>The Wisdom of Dawn Key</h1><p><em>A man, a donkey, and a serious misunderstanding about who was being stubborn.</em></p><p>Let us call her Dawn Key. Balaam\u2019s donkey was female, and she deserves a name. She had carried him faithfully for years, knew his ways, and probably knew more of his business than he would have liked.</p><p>Then came the morning of the important journey. A king named Balak was frightened of Israel. He wanted Balaam to come and curse them, and he was prepared to pay well for the service. God had already told Balaam that these people were blessed. Balak wanted them cursed anyway. That ought to tell you something about how this trip was going to go.</p><p>Eventually, Balaam set out with permission to go, but with a firm instruction: he could say only what God told him. Along the way, an angel of the Lord stood in the road with a drawn sword. Dawn saw him. Balaam did not.</p><p>Dawn had never attended a school of prophecy, but she understood enough about swords to know that walking into one was a poor travel plan. She turned into a field. Balaam beat her and steered her back onto the road.</p><p>Farther along, the angel stood between two vineyard walls. Dawn squeezed aside, pressing Balaam\u2019s foot against the stone. That earned her another beating. Finally, they reached a place so narrow that she could neither turn nor squeeze past. She lay down. Balaam lost his temper and struck her again.</p><p>Then God enabled the donkey to speak. Dawn asked what she had done to deserve three beatings.</p><p>Now, I believe I would have paused right there. I might have forgotten about my foot. I would certainly have had several questions concerning the donkey. Balaam, however, answered her. He was so angry about being made to look foolish that he entered into an argument with his transportation.</p><p>He told her that if he had a sword, he would kill her. There was already a sword nearby. Dawn was trying very hard to keep him from meeting it.</p><p>She reminded him that she was the same donkey he had ridden for years. Had she ever behaved this way before? He admitted she had not. Then the Lord opened Balaam\u2019s eyes, and suddenly the view from the donkey\u2019s back looked quite different. There stood the angel, sword in hand. Balaam fell facedown.</p><p>The angel demanded to know why he had beaten her. Three times she had turned aside. If she had kept going, Balaam would have died, and the donkey would have lived. The animal he had punished for delaying him had been keeping him alive.</p><p>I hope he apologized. Personally, I think she had earned the rest of the day off.</p><p>Balaam continued his journey with the warning ringing in his ears: speak only what God tells you. When he reached Balak, the king took him up to look over Israel. Balaam blessed them. Balak was furious. He had ordered a curse.</p><p>So the king tried another location. Apparently, he thought God might give a different answer from another hill. Another blessing. They tried again, and still another blessing followed. Three times Dawn had tried to stop Balaam on the road. Three times Balak tried to get a curse out of a blessing. Dawn had a better grasp of when to stop.</p><p>But the story leaves us with something more serious than a talking donkey. Balaam could deliver God\u2019s words, yet the later Scriptures describe his greed and his part in leading Israel into sin. That troubles me. A man can say the right thing and still want the wrong thing.</p><p>A king can ask what God says while hoping to hear only what he has already decided. And I can become so certain about where I am going that I get angry at anything that slows me down.</p><p>That is where Dawn Key gets my attention. Before I complain about the interruption, I ought to ask the Lord whether there is something I need to see. On that road, the donkey saw what Balaam could not. And when she finally spoke, the man had plenty to say.</p><p>He needed to listen.</p><section aria-labelledby=\"scripture-references\"><h2 id=\"scripture-references\">Scripture references</h2><p>This is a humorous retelling of the biblical account. Dawn Key\u2019s name and the narrator\u2019s imagined observations are storytelling additions.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Numbers%2022%3A1-20&amp;version=KJV\">Numbers 22:1\u201320</a> \u2014 Balak\u2019s request and God\u2019s instructions to Balaam.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Numbers%2022%3A21-35&amp;version=KJV\">Numbers 22:21\u201335</a> \u2014 The donkey, the angel, and Balaam\u2019s opened eyes.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Numbers%2022%3A36-24%3A25&amp;version=KJV\">Numbers 22:36\u201324:25</a> \u2014 Balaam blesses Israel instead of cursing them.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Numbers%2031%3A16&amp;version=KJV\">Numbers 31:16</a> \u2014 Balaam\u2019s part in leading Israel away from faithfulness.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=2%20Peter%202%3A15-16&amp;version=KJV\">2 Peter 2:15\u201316</a> \u2014 Balaam\u2019s greed and the donkey\u2019s rebuke.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Jude%201%3A11&amp;version=KJV\">Jude 1:11</a> \u2014 A warning about Balaam\u2019s pursuit of reward.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Revelation%202%3A14&amp;version=KJV\">Revelation 2:14</a> \u2014 Christ\u2019s warning about the teaching of Balaam.</li></ul></section><nav class=\"reading\" aria-label=\"Reading navigation\"><a href=\"/testimonies\">Our Shared Journey</a><a href=\"/index.html\">Main Menu</a></nav></main></body></html>";
