 const articles=[{"title": "Just What He Ought to Be", "slug": "/just-what-he-ought-to-be", "subtitle": "The gifts and burdens we receive, and what we carry forward", "body": "<p>My dad had very little formal education. But he had an earned PhD from the University of Hard Knocks.</p>\n<p>UHK. You can imagine the school colors.</p>\n<p>He was an intelligent man, and he gave me things that other people never receive. Through what God had done in his life, blessings reached into mine.</p>\n<p>He also had a saying I have never forgotten. When he admired someone, he would say:</p>\n<p>“He is just what he ought to be.”</p>\n<p>I would love for my dad to look at me now and say that.</p>\n<p>There are things about me, though, that I wish were different.</p>\n<p>My temper, for instance. I can go from calm to hair-on-fire in what feels like a nanosecond. It is something I struggle with, and something God is helping me address.</p>\n<p>I received more from my father than the things I admire. Some of what came into my life has been burdensome. I believe he would never knowingly have handed me something that would hurt me.</p>\n<p>Yet here we are. We pass along more than we intend.</p>\n<p>Years ago, I read about research into epigenetics. One account particularly caught my attention: mice exposed to a scent paired with an unpleasant experience had descendants that responded differently to that scent, even without having undergone the same training themselves.</p>\n<p>That gave me something to ponder.</p>\n<p>What might reach into a life before that person has any opportunity to choose it?</p>\n<p>We already recognize some of this in families. We receive physical characteristics and tendencies. We learn ways of speaking, loving, fearing, and responding. Some lessons are taught deliberately. Others are absorbed before we know we are learning.</p>\n<p>The research made me look again at questions I already carried into Scripture, including the unusual account of Jacob’s flocks in Genesis 30. I began wondering about the reach of one generation into another, and about how much happens beyond my understanding.</p>\n<p>My questions also turned toward the spiritual lives of those who came before me. What had my father cherished? My grandfather? What had shaped their trust, their fears, their choices? Could something they welcomed or endured have consequences reaching into circumstances I never chose?</p>\n<p>These are questions I bring before God. I cannot trace every difficulty in my life to its beginning. I do not need to assign my father blame in order to acknowledge that I inherited things I struggle with.</p>\n<p>Nor do I carry his guilt. The passage about fathers eating sour grapes and their children’s teeth being set on edge brings us to God’s insistence upon personal responsibility. —<a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Ezekiel+18%3A1-4%2C19-20&amp;version=KJV\">Ezekiel 18:1–4, 19–20</a></p>\n<p>But I still have to live honestly with what is present in me.</p>\n<p>If anger rises quickly, I cannot simply say, “That came from Dad,” and leave someone else to suffer it. I can bring it to God. I can acknowledge the hurt I cause, seek forgiveness, and ask Him to help me respond differently.</p>\n<p>I have found that there are things I do not recognize until I become willing to look at them with Him.</p>\n<p>Humility matters there. So does honesty—with God, with myself, and with the people and creatures entrusted to my care. I want to remain teachable enough to discover something I had not known to ask.</p>\n<p>Sometimes understanding comes slowly.</p>\n<p>I think of Mary keeping things and pondering them in her heart. Some things needed to be carried before they could be understood. —<a href=\"https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke+2%3A19%2C51&amp;version=KJV\">Luke 2:19, 51</a></p>\n<p>I have things like that.</p>\n<p>Some of what I write may be difficult to understand. Sometimes it is difficult for me to express. I am inviting you to consider that our familiar explanations may leave much unexplored.</p>\n<p>Think outside your skin for a moment.</p>\n<p>You are living a life whose beginnings reach farther back than your memory. Your influence may reach farther forward than you will ever see. And as a Christian, I believe our lives belong within an eternal purpose whose depth we have scarcely begun to discover.</p>\n<p>When I contemplate creation, I picture the generosity of God’s love finding expression in a universe filled with lives He knows. That is the wonder behind these writings: the One whose greatness exceeds my understanding desires that we know Him.</p>\n<p>What a God.</p>\n<p>I love my dad. I am grateful for what he gave me. And I am grateful that God is still working with what has come into my hands.</p>\n<p>I want to cherish the good. I want His help with what causes harm. I want those who come after me to receive something better because I was willing to let Him work in me.</p>\n<p>“He is just what he ought to be.”</p>\n<p>I am still growing toward those words.</p>", "refs": [["Ezekiel 18:1–4, 19–20", "Ezekiel+18%3A1-4%2C19-20", "ezekiel", "Ezekiel"], ["Luke 2:19, 51", "Luke+2%3A19%2C51", "luke", "Luke"]]}];

